WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.358
The Develop phase is where the plan becomes materials.

00:00:03.358 --> 00:00:07.340
Course creators write narration, refine screen text, create slides, build graphics,

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:11.322
record audio, edit video, assemble scenarios, configure interactions, create job aids,

00:00:11.322 --> 00:00:12.408
and build quizzes.

00:00:12.408 --> 00:00:14.579
This is the most visible phase,

00:00:14.579 --> 00:00:18.923
but it works best when the analysis and design work have already

00:00:18.923 --> 00:00:21.095
clarified what needs to be built.

00:00:21.095 --> 00:00:24.715
Development should begin with stable content and a manageable prototype.

00:00:24.715 --> 00:00:29.421
A prototype might include a representative module, a sample interaction, a narration treatment,

00:00:29.421 --> 00:00:31.231
or a technical publishing test.

00:00:31.231 --> 00:00:35.213
The point is to validate structure, tone, visual style, accessibility approach,

00:00:35.213 --> 00:00:38.832
and technical behavior before the team invests in full production.

00:00:38.832 --> 00:00:42.452
Prototyping reduces the chance of rebuilding the entire course later.

00:00:42.452 --> 00:00:44.262
Asset organization matters during development.

00:00:44.262 --> 00:00:47.520
Use clear folder structures, module-based file names, version notes,

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.778
and a distinction between source files and final exports.

00:00:50.778 --> 00:00:54.398
Keep narration scripts, audio files, caption files, graphics, authoring files,

00:00:54.398 --> 00:00:55.846
and review notes traceable.

00:00:55.846 --> 00:00:59.104
Maintainability is not something to worry about after launch;

00:00:59.104 --> 00:01:02.000
it starts while the materials are being created.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.171
Review cycles should be managed deliberately.

00:01:04.171 --> 00:01:06.343
Subject matter experts can verify accuracy,

00:01:06.343 --> 00:01:08.877
instructional reviewers can check flow and alignment,

00:01:08.877 --> 00:01:10.687
accessibility reviewers can identify barriers,

00:01:10.687 --> 00:01:13.221
and technical reviewers can test publishing behavior.

00:01:13.221 --> 00:01:16.479
Too many unstructured review comments can slow the project,

00:01:16.479 --> 00:01:21.185
so define review roles and the type of feedback needed at each checkpoint.

00:01:21.185 --> 00:01:23.719
Avoid overproduction before the design is validated.

00:01:23.719 --> 00:01:28.063
Highly polished videos, custom interactions, or detailed graphics are expensive to change.

00:01:28.063 --> 00:01:30.234
Build enough to test the idea,

00:01:30.234 --> 00:01:34.578
then commit to final production when the team agrees the design works.

00:01:34.578 --> 00:01:37.474
The goal of development is not maximum complexity;

00:01:37.474 --> 00:01:42.180
it is purposeful material that learners can use and the organization can maintain.
